/**
@page project_overview Project overview

@tableofcontents

@section overview_identity What libopenwch is

libopenwch is a driver library for WCH RISC-V microcontrollers.  It is
deliberately modelled on libopencm3 in four dimensions:

<table>
<tr><th>Dimension</th><th>What that means</th></tr>
<tr><td>API style</td><td>lower-case <code>periph_verb_noun()</code> functions,
the peripheral base address as the first argument, constants named
<code>PERIPH_REGISTER_BIT</code>, and types ending in <code>_t</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Directory layout</td><td><code>include/libopenwch/&lt;family&gt;/</code>,
<code>lib/&lt;family&gt;/</code>, a <code>qingke/</code> core layer analogous to
<code>cm3/</code>, and a <code>dispatch/</code> header layer.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Build system</td><td>recursive <code>TARGETS</code>, reusable
<code>mk/</code> modules, <code>ld/devices.data</code> plus
<code>genlink.py</code>, and generated <code>nvic.h</code> from
<code>irq.json</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Code conventions</td><td>clang-format is the formatting authority;
<code>checkpatch.pl</code> is a second opinion; generated files are never
edited by hand.</td></tr>
</table>

The archive is the only library product: one archive per family, plus an
optional mini-libc archive.  An application includes the reusable
<code>mk/</code> modules, selects a device, and links the matching archive.

@section overview_example A first call

@code
#include <libopenwch/ch32v0/gpio.h>
#include <libopenwch/ch32v0/rcc.h>

int main(void)
{
gpio_set_mode(GPIOC, GPIO_MODE_OUT_PP, GPIO1 | GPIO2);
for (;;) {
gpio_toggle(GPIOC, GPIO1 | GPIO2);
}
}
@endcode

The same call shape is used by every driver: the first argument is the
peripheral base address.

@section overview_ecosystem The four repositories

| Repository | Role | Relationship |
| --- | --- | --- |
| <a href="https://github.com/LrkSeraph/libopenwch">libopenwch</a> | the driver library | this documentation set |
| <a href="https://github.com/LrkSeraph/libopenwch-template">libopenwch-template</a> | application skeleton | submodules: library and flasher |
| <a href="https://github.com/LrkSeraph/libopenwch-examples">libopenwch-examples</a> | five worked examples and CI integration tests | submodule: library |
| <a href="https://github.com/LrkSeraph/libopenwch-tools">libopenwch-tools</a> | <code>wchlink</code>, the WCH-LinkE flasher | referenced by the template |

The four repositories can be cloned and built independently.  The library
repository itself has no submodules and needs only a RISC-V toolchain.

@section overview_references Design references

WCH's official EVT packages for the CH32V003 and CH583 were consulted as
register- and timing-level reference material.  The implementation is original
and uses different names and structure; no EVT code is copied.  ch32fun
influenced the toolchain strategy only.  See @ref licensing_and_provenance for
the full provenance summary.

@section overview_api_style One deliberate API deviation

On CH32V00x, `gpio_set_mode(port, nibble, pins)` takes one opaque four-bit
CNF/MODE nibble rather than libopencm3's `(mode, cnf)` pair.  The nibble is not
a bit-field decomposition: exhaustive enumeration of all two-bit field
positions shows no mapping that reproduces WCH's documented values.  The
documented encoding is therefore preserved as an opaque value.
*/
